+# Request spec performance: phase 2
+
+## Scope and reproduction
+
+Run **only** `spec/requests`, sequentially. Do not include system/streaming specs,
+change factories, or run a combined models + requests command. Existing `js`,
+`search`, and `streaming` exclusions stay enabled. The model-phase changes in the
+working tree are preserved.
+
+```sh
+PROFILE=1 docs/profile-requests.sh requests-entry 12345
+PROFILE=1 VERIFY_SETUP=1 docs/profile-requests.sh requests-shared 12345
+VERIFY_SETUP=1 docs/profile-requests.sh requests-audit-final 67890
+bundle exec stackprof tmp/rspec-profile/requests-entry.stackprof --text --limit 30
+```
+
+Labels name artifacts, not code configurations: capture a baseline before making
+changes. Outputs (`.log`, `.json`, optional `.stackprof`) go to
+`tmp/rspec-profile`. For command-time-limited environments launch one invocation
+with `nohup env PROFILE=1 docs/profile-requests.sh LABEL SEED > /tmp/request-profile-run.log 2>&1 &`
+and poll its log. Never run benchmarks concurrently.
+
+Environment matches phase 1: Ruby 4.0.6, Rails 8.1.3.1, local PostgreSQL/Redis,
+no parallelization or coverage, 1 ms StackProf CPU sampling, seed 12345.
+
+## Baseline
+
+No earlier request timing was available in the supplied phase-1 notes/artifacts.
+The request-entry baseline is measured after model optimization. However, model
+logging/job hooks are type-scoped and do not affect requests; the removed
+file-load verbose-query assignment lived in a model spec that is not loaded by
+`rspec spec/requests`. Thus the isolated request configuration is unchanged from
+before phase 1. Do not equate this with running requests after loading all specs.
+
+| Configuration | RSpec execution | Wall incl. boot | Examples | Failures |
+| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
+| Request entry baseline | 174.95 s | 178.19 s | 1,877 | 0 |
+| Quiet request logging | 154.65 s | 157.79 s | 1,877 | 0 |
+| Queued jobs experiment, before inline exceptions | 103.60 s | 106.91 s | 1,877 | 41 |
+| Scoped jobs + inline exceptions + state audit | 115.25 s | 119.22 s | 1,877 | 0 |
+| Final: also share caching matrix setup + state audit | 99.93 s | 103.41 s | 1,877 | 0 |
+| Final, seed 67890, no sampler, full isolation audit | 99.74 s | 102.78 s | 1,877 | 0 |
+
+Baseline CPU profile (inclusive, overlapping sample percentages):
+
+- Fabrication: 60.3%; do not change factories in this phase.
+- Sidekiq inline push: 30.8%; user webhook callback alone: 14.3%.
+- SQL log subscriber: 8.6%; IO writes: 2.1% self samples.
+- Setup hooks: 62.7%.
+
+Slow files by summed example runtime: caching 34.36 s (258 examples), ActivityPub
+replies 16.53 s (45), v2 notifications 8.18 s (24), signature verification 6.71 s
+(30), v1 notifications 5.61 s (17). Shared API examples account for another
+14.86 s across 239 examples (JSON attributes these to their definition file).
+
+## Results and changes
+
+The final CPU-profiled request run is **42.9% shorter (1.75x faster)** in RSpec
+execution and **42.0% shorter (1.72x faster)** including boot. The 41 experimental
+failures are not accepted regressions: they identified necessary inline work.
+
+Using the separately measured phase-1 model results (156.60 -> 55.40 s), combined
+execution is **331.55 -> 155.33 s (2.13x faster)**. Including boot for both
+independent invocations: **337.68 -> 161.68 s (2.09x faster)**. This is a sum of
+separate measurements, not a whole-suite run or a guarantee of identical CI
+wall times. No combined spec invocation was run.
+
+### 1. Scoped request logging
+
+`spec/support/request_performance.rb` limits Rails/ActiveRecord logging to WARN
+around setup, execution, and teardown. It preserves warnings/errors and restores
+original logger levels in `ensure`. `:verbose_logging` opts an example/group out
+(for assertions on logging or debugging); it preserves the incoming level, not
+necessarily DEBUG. RSpec output is unaffected. Non-request logging is unchanged.
+
+### 2. Scoped queue-only jobs
+
+Requests use `Sidekiq.testing!(:fake) { ... }` by default. This still runs model
+callbacks and enqueues real jobs with real arguments; it does not stub services,
+workers, or responses. `rspec-sidekiq` clears job queues before each example.
+The block API restores the ambient mode even on failure. The global inline hook
+now skips requests as well as models; all other types retain their old behavior.
+
+Use `sidekiq: :inline` when setup or assertions need worker effects. Exceptions:
+
+- Notification count/list/account setups and conversations: fanout/notifications.
+- Populated home timeline: distribution workers populate Redis feeds.
+- Unfavourite endpoint: removal is asynchronous.
+- Report and moderation notification examples: actual mail delivery.
+- Both negative admin mail assertions: keep mail execution enabled so an
+  unintended delivery can still fail the examples.
+
+Existing imperative `Sidekiq.testing!(:fake)` calls became `sidekiq: :fake`
+metadata. This also supports overriding an enclosing inline group. Do not use
+imperative switches or nested testing blocks inside request examples: global
+switches do not override the active thread-local mode, and nested blocks are
+unsupported by Sidekiq. Keep setup requiring worker effects inside a tagged
+group, including negative/empty-result examples in that group.
+
+### 3. Transactional shared request setup
+
+The caching matrix rebuilt an identical graph for all 258 examples. It now uses
+TestProf's `before_all` transaction (already an available test dependency) rather
+than a bare `before(:all)`. Each example still has Rails' own rollback boundary,
+then the group transaction rolls back. The existing `let` lookups return fresh
+records, so Ruby instances and association caches are not shared. Requests,
+authentication, Redis/cache cleanup, CSRF handling, and every assertion remain
+per-example and unchanged. No factories were edited.
+
+Context setup is outside per-example hooks, so the shared graph has an explicit
+scoped fake-job block. This avoids relying on whichever spec type ran previously.
+Do not move Redis-dependent setup, mocks, or request execution into `before_all`:
+only this fixed database graph is shared.
+
+Caching examples went from 34.36 s summed runtime at baseline to 4.18 s finally;
+suite duration (which includes context setup) is the primary comparison, not
+summed example timings alone.
+
+## Validation
+
+Both final seeds (12345 and 67890) pass all 1,877 examples. RuboCop passes on
+all 28 Ruby files changed/added in this phase; Bash syntax and `git diff --check`
+also pass.
+
+All baseline/final JSON reports contain the same 1,877 stable example IDs, with
+no new pending/excluded examples and no assertion changes. The optional
+`docs/verify_request_setup.rb`, loaded before specs with `VERIFY_SETUP=1`, checks:
+
+- Active Sidekiq mode and quiet logger levels before every request example.
+- Restoration of ambient Sidekiq mode and logger levels after every example.
+- Database snapshots before/after caching examples, after their transaction
+  rollback, and absence of the fixed shared graph after the suite.
+
+Additional checks:
+
+- All 258 caching examples pass with seed 67890 and full database snapshots.
+  Compare `attributes_before_type_cast`: an initial audit using `attributes`
+  falsely flagged all caching examples because deserialized `UserSettings`
+  objects have identity-based equality. A single-example diff confirmed identical
+  stored settings. This was corrected in the audit, not worked around in specs.
+- A filtered Health check request passed with `sidekiq: :inline` and
+  `:verbose_logging`, starting from ambient disabled Sidekiq mode and DEBUG
+  logger levels. The outer audit verified both were restored.
+- A temporary `before` hook deliberately failed the same filtered request with
+  fake jobs/quiet logging. Exactly that failure was reported; no state-leak or
+  after-suite errors occurred. Probe hooks were loaded from `/tmp`, not added to
+  the normal suite. Use `rspec spec/requests --example 'Health check endpoint'`
+  with `--require ./docs/verify_request_setup.rb` before probe hooks to repeat.
+
+## Next phase
+
+Final inclusive CPU samples: Fabrication 45.5%, Sidekiq test push 11.1%,
+PostStatusService 6.2%, BCrypt hashing 3.9%. These overlap and are not additive.
+Remaining slow files by summed example time: ActivityPub replies 9.15 s,
+v2 notifications 6.59 s, signature verification 6.52 s, v1 notifications 4.65 s.
+Shared API example definitions total another 10.27 s. Retain worker execution
+where the specs assert its effects. Factory improvements are intentionally
+left to the next phase, not hidden via stubbing application behavior here.
